# js2py textbook authoring rules

## Reader and outcome

The reader is an experienced frontend engineer, comfortable with JavaScript,
Promises/async-await and basic TypeScript, but new to implementing backends,
databases and operations. Do not assume Express, SQL, authentication-system or
server-administration experience. Do not require advanced type gymnastics.

The outcome is independent implementation, testing, deployment and maintenance
of a small backend. Reading all pages or copying a demo is not sufficient.

## Teaching depth

Follow the full authoring standard:
/Users/mikmyp/Documents/code-projects/langshift.dev/docs/js2py-authoring-standard.zh-cn.md

Teach motivation, prerequisites, conceptual mechanisms, data/control flow,
responsibility boundaries, useful frontend analogies AND their limits, complete
examples, predicted output, failure diagnosis, graduated exercises, independent
answers and measurable acceptance. An API inventory is not a lesson.

Do not abbreviate essential backend foundations because the reader has frontend
experience. Do not inflate chapters with irrelevant advanced mechanisms either.
Introduce unfamiliar concepts before using them. Explain why important behavior
occurs, not merely translate each source-code line into prose.

## Prerequisite contracts

Use /Users/mikmyp/Documents/code-projects/langshift.dev/docs/js2py-chapter-contracts.json
before writing each chapter. State prerequisites, new capabilities, independent
acceptance and exclusions. Required exercises must not rely on unexplained later
material. Introductory chapter 00 is a fully explained small script, not a full
reproducible project. Engineering mastery belongs after language/module/file/test
foundations. Keep the retained historical lab out of the introductory exit gate.

## Code and environments

- For JS/Python browser comparisons, use PythonEditor with compare={true}.
- Use canRun={false} for local-only or static comparisons. State why.
- Configuration, shell commands and complete local files may use Code or ordinary
  fenced blocks rendered by the site's Code Hike pipeline.
- A server, database connection, multi-file program or TypeScript type check is
  not browser JavaScript/Pyodide just because it is displayed in an editor.
- Complete examples are self-contained; never depend on earlier editor state.
- Identify expected errors and exercise starters explicitly. Do not present
  ellipses or pass as a complete project implementation.
- Shared lab files are the source of truth for displayed and downloadable code.
- State filenames, dependencies, working directory, commands, inputs, expected
  behavior and limitations. Test in the claimed environment.

## Language and maintenance

Respond in the user's requested language. The current teaching draft is Chinese;
maintain equivalent English and traditional Chinese editions. Keep executable
snippets, filenames and expected results stable across translations.

Use a coherent tested version baseline. Reference primary documentation for
technical claims. Do not invent language performance comparisons or benchmark
claims when they are unrelated to the lesson.

Preserve existing chapter URLs. Report completed work, verification and remaining
limitations; do not claim unrevised chapters or untested examples are finished.
